### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as
`src/**/*.test.ts`. `npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node,
with coverage measured over `src/`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A test drives the game over its own clock for a counted number of frames, so
it needs no browser, and `@napi-rs/canvas` is installed for a test that
wants a real 2D context.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
A test stands the engine up over its own canvas and clock and advances the
game a counted number of frames, so it needs no browser, and
`@napi-rs/canvas` is installed to supply the 2D context.
{{else}}
A test stands the engine up over a canvas from `@napi-rs/canvas` and a
surface and clock of its own, then advances the game a counted number of
frames with `engine.advance`, so it needs no browser.
{{/if}}

### The checks the repository passes

Four commands run over the finished repository before it is reviewed:

| Command | Checks |
| --- | --- |
| `npx tsc --noEmit` | The code type-checks. A repository that does not compile is not reviewed. |
| `npx eslint .` | The code is lint-clean. |
| `npx prettier --check .` | The code is formatted. |
| `npx vitest run --coverage` | Your tests pass, with their count and coverage reported. |

They run on the repository as you leave it. `npm run typecheck`,
`npm run lint`, `npm run format`, and `npm test` run the same four locally.

### The build interface

`npm ci` followed by `npm run build` produces the complete static site into
`dist/` at the repository root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory as the entry point. That directory runs correctly when served
as-is, both at the root of a static file server and mounted under a
sub-path, so every URL the build emits or constructs at runtime is
page-relative rather than root-absolute. The scripts that do this are
already in `package.json`.

The build is self-contained. The asset tools on this machine's `PATH` are
development tools for producing the files `specs/assets.md` lists and
locates; the produced files are committed, the build bundles them, and
neither `npm ci` nor `npm run build` invokes a tool.

## Goal of this build

Produce a complete, polished, playable game that runs entirely in a browser,
and the art and sound it plays. This is a substantial front-end build: a
rules-exact simulation with simultaneous motion and collision, a
direct-manipulation editor with drags, tapes, and undo, a course of
challenges you design yourself, a presentation that makes a machine of
spinning arms worth watching, and a produced set of sprites, effects, and
sounds. Aim for a game a person would enjoy playing and replaying for better
records.

The build ships both of the game's modes, chosen from the title menu:
Campaign, the course of challenges you author to
`specs/modes/campaign.md`, and Extras, the fixed shelf of
`specs/modes/extras.md`. The field, the parts, the simulation, the editor,
and the debug surface the other spec files describe are the same in both.

