## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the
simulation and the editor's rules.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions and sizes in this specification are given in the game's own
logical units on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`, 16:9).
The origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y`
increases downward. The stage center is `(STAGE_CX, STAGE_CY)`
(`640, 360`). Where the field sits within the stage, and where each hex's
center falls, are given in `specs/field.md`; the editor's regions are given
in `specs/editor.md`.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the
  aspect ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The
  complete stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and
  at any pixel density.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the
  runtime alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.
  The pointer position the game is given is in those same units, so a hit
  test against a hex center needs no conversion.

Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in
seconds, integrated against the delta time the runtime hands each update. A
run's own unit is the cycle, and `specs/simulation.md` fixes how cycles
advance against that delta time.

## Visual design

The sky is dark. The look is yours to art-direct within what
`specs/assets.md` fixes about the produced art: the palette, the typography,
how a mote, a filament, an arm, and each sigil's engraving are drawn, and
how a running machine moves and glows.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads everything the legibility lists
fix: the mote classes and types in `specs/field.md`, the parts and their
working state in `specs/parts.md`, the editor's selection, ghosts, cursor,
and tape glyphs in `specs/editor.md`, the art and sound bars in
`specs/assets.md`, and every piece of text on the stage. The palette, type,
glow, and every other aspect of the look are yours.
